Subject: register VM: extend the in-place string accumulator to chained concatenation

What this does
  A `'a' + x + 'b'` chain allocates two strings in the register VM where the
  stack VM allocates one. Filling a 3-part leaf string in Octane's splay (`'String
  for key ' + tag + ' in leaf node'`) allocated 7.80M strings over the run versus
  3.96M in the stack VM; the same doubling shows on any chained concatenation.

  The register VM appended in place only when the add's destination register was
  also its left operand (the `s += x` shape that patch 0178 covers). For a chain,
  the intermediate result is a separate register, the add's right operand is a
  constant, and that constant reaches the interpreter through the non-pool branch
  of `regvm_t_binary_cpool` / `L_ROP_binary_cpool`, which had no accumulator call
  at all. Even where the accumulator was tried, the intermediate had no headroom
  to extend into because the register VM never set the stack VM's scatter hint,
  so `JS_ConcatString1` allocated the result at its exact length.

  Sets `js_scat_hint` in the register add before the engine call, and gives the
  non-pool constant branches of both `binary_cpool` handlers the same
  `regvm_accum_append` call the pool branch already had. A chain now allocates its
  first result once and extends it in place.

Why we need it
  R7 of `.local/regvm-fix-plan.md`. The register VM loses to the stack VM on
  splay, and the plan asked first whether the register VM frees more objects than
  the stack VM. It does not: object, array and bytecode allocation and free
  counts are identical (class[1] 8,255,858 objects, class[2] 3,840,008 arrays,
  free_object 12,097,459 on both). The one producer that does differ is the
  string allocator, and the extra strings are also what drove the deferred
  reference-counting parking and reconcile traffic that made splay's teardown
  look expensive (js_drc_zero 36.7M calls vs 0.7M). Fixing the producer removes
  both costs at once.

What it adds
  js_regvm_binary now sets the scatter hint before an ADD.
  regvm_t_binary_cpool and L_ROP_binary_cpool now call regvm_accum_append on
  their non-pool constant branch, exactly as their pool branch does.
  quickjs-regvm-step.inc regenerated to match.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No. The register opcodes are unchanged; only the runtime add path differs.

Notes
  - The accumulator refuses a constant left operand and a string that any slot
    owns, so the first concatenation of a chain still allocates once and only a
    register-only intermediate is extended. `var t = 'x' + tag; var r = t + 'y'`
    still leaves `t` alone.
  - Measured on the deterministic corpus (instructions:u, same compiler, A/B):
    splay -3.66%, cycles -2.8%; every other row within +0.28% (pdfjs) and
    -0.06%/+0.06% (noise). splay's instruction ratio to the stack VM goes
    1.108 -> 1.068 and its cycle ratio 1.042 -> 1.015.
  - box2d checksum 546.1008391043414 on threaded, monolith and STEPCHECK builds;
    differential suite 400/0/0.

diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -23994,6 +23994,11 @@
     ops[1] = js_dup(b);
     switch (subop) {
     case REG_BIN_ADD:
+        /* Mirror the stack VM's scatter hint: a concatenation result that is
+           itself the left operand of the next add should carry headroom so that
+           add can extend it in place (JS_ConcatString1's slack). Without this
+           every chained concat allocates a fresh string. */
+        js_scat_hint = 1;
         if (js_add_slow(ctx, ops + 2))
             goto fail;
         break;
@@ -26967,6 +26972,18 @@
